{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>On the Day of Atonement</b> — From the mere fact that Scripture says \"on the Day of Atonement,\" I would not know that it is on the tenth of the month. If so, why is it said \"on the tenth of the month\"? Rather, the blowing on the tenth of the month overrides Shabbat throughout all your land, whereas the teru'ah of Rosh HaShanah does not override Shabbat throughout all your land but in the court alone — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 25:9). Now, the Rav, owing to his expertise in the Talmud, with everything before him like a set table, is not concerned and cites the baraitot verbatim, and they mislead other people. For it is known and clear in the Gemara (Rosh HaShanah 29b) that all the tekia'ot of Rosh HaShanah and Yom HaKippurim, and even optional ones, are permitted on Shabbat, because it is a skill and not a melakhah, and they would blow by Torah law on Shabbat of Rosh HaShanah and of Yom HaKippurim in every place. And the matter of blowing in a court is an enactment of Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai from when the Temple was destroyed, because they decreed concerning it since everyone is obligated in the blowing of the shofar and not everyone is expert in blowing the shofar — perhaps he will take it in his hand and go to an expert to learn, and he will carry it four cubits in the public domain, and Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai permitted it to us in a court alone. And this has no root in the meaning of the verse whatsoever. And what they mentioned (Sifra, parashah 2:5) — \"the blowing on the tenth overrides Shabbat\" — is to say that it is performed on every Yom HaKippurim, for the overriding on Yom HaKippurim that falls on Shabbat and that which falls on a weekday is one and the same, since Yom HaKippurim is equivalent to Shabbat for all melakhah, both for carrying and for all melakhot. Nevertheless, the Rav wished to teach us that the blowing of the shofar on Yom HaKippurim is performed in every place, for the meaning of \"you shall sound the shofar throughout all your land\" is to teach that every individual is obligated to blow, and the blowing is not in the court alone like the counting."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The Rav refers to Maimonides; Ramban disputes his citation of baraitot that appear to contradict the established Gemara ruling in Rosh HaShanah 29b."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:59:41.702112281Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}